 \section{Discussion and Conclusions}\label{sec:discussion}
-DGF reconstructs black hole ringdown waveforms by combining time--frequency information with signed multiscale temporal features. A shared encoder, joint-channel synthesis and multiscale residual reconstruction estimate the clean LISA $A/E$ waveforms from the noisy observation and gap mask. For the 5810 test sources with $\rho\ge10$ and 32--52-s gaps, the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch is 0.00510. The mean over all 6,000 independent sources is 0.00821. The waveform comparisons show recovery of the decaying oscillations across intervals containing the signal peak, while gap-local and spectral errors quantify amplitude-sensitive deviations.
+DGF reconstructs black hole ringdown waveforms by combining time--frequency information with signed multiscale temporal features. A shared encoder, joint-channel synthesis and multiscale residual reconstruction estimate the clean LISA $A/E$ waveforms from the noisy observation and gap mask. For the 5810 test sources with $\rho\ge10$ and 32--52-s gaps, the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch is 0.00510. The mean over all 6,000 independent sources is 0.00821. The waveform comparisons show recovery of the oscillations across the gaps and their subsequent decay, while gap-local and spectral errors quantify amplitude-sensitive deviations.
 
 The signal-basis comparison connects representation accuracy with coefficient estimation from noisy observations. The small clean-target projection error shows that the rank-128 basis resolves the simulated waveform family. DGF combines learned coefficients with a multiscale residual to recover the waveform from temporal, phase and noise features. Its mean mismatch is lower than that of the regularized basis fit on the same observations. For the 1824 sources with $\rho\ge10$ and the joint envelope peak inside the gap, the mean envelope-weighted phase error within the gap is $4.90^\circ$. This statistic measures phase recovery throughout the suppressed interval, complementing the peak-phase error and full-record mismatch.
 
